#clinical-finding(title: [Repeated Handgrip Dynamometry Quantifies a Force and Recovery Deficit])[
Two-session handgrip strength (HGS) testing with a digital dynamometer detects a reproducible performance deficit in ME/CFS across age bands, using a shared protocol family: repeated maximal grips in two sessions separated by about 60 minutes. Grip count, rest intervals, and dynamometer model vary between cohorts.

- In 105 adults meeting Canadian Consensus Criteria, maximal (Fmax) and mean (Fmean) grip force were lower than in 66 healthy controls (p \< 0.0001); the same cohort included 18 patients with cancer-related fatigue, who serve as a deconditioning-relevant comparator. The within-session fatigue ratio (Fmax/Fmean) was higher (p ≤ 0.0012), and the 60-minute recovery ratio (Fmean session 2 / session 1) was lower (p ≤ 0.0020) @Jakel2021HGS.
- In 147 children and young adults (10–25 y) referred with suspected ME/CFS and 83 healthy controls, patients generated 9.93 kg less grip force (95% CI −12.00 to −7.85 kg). Recovery ratio differed between ME/CFS and controls @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP.
- In 102 patients with post-COVID syndrome (61.4% meeting ME/CFS criteria), mean HGS was lower, fatigability was higher, and recovery was poorer in patients with ME/CFS than in those without (p = 0.031, 0.042, 0.048) @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.

*Replication status:* partially replicated — two independent ME/CFS cohorts (adult CCC; pediatric/young adult) plus one post-COVID cohort, all with consistent direction and comparable effect size for absolute force.

*Evidence type:* prospective observational cohort studies. *Severity applicability:* mild and moderate ambulatory patients only. Severe and very severe: unknown — no study stratified by severity, and repeated maximal-effort testing may be infeasible or provoking in this group.

*Certainty: 0.60.* (Prospective cohorts: two ME/CFS cohorts of 105 and 147 patients plus a post-COVID cohort of 102 patients; consistent direction; no pre-registered threshold, no external validation, and protocols differ in grip count, rest intervals, and device.)

*Measurement caveat:* the within-session fatigue ratio is not statistically independent of the elevated within-subject variability reported in the adult cohort — both are functions of how scattered the ten force values are @Popkirov2025CV. A high fatigue ratio should be read as "force declined and varied across trials", not as a clean physiological fatigue slope (see @oq:handgrip-peripheral-vs-central).

*Consequence:* A clinician can objectify functional limitation with a cheap handheld device in about 15 minutes, but the test documents impairment — it does not explain the impairment and it does not establish the diagnosis.
] <cf:handgrip-force-deficit>
